---
import LearningLayout from '../../../layouts/LearningLayout.astro';
import Icon from '../../../components/Icon.astro';
import { foodSyntheses } from '../../../lib/food-evidence';
const overall = foodSyntheses[0];
---
<LearningLayout title="What changes access to healthier food?" intro="Explore a worked example linking study findings, local constraints and a household scenario. Every study, finding and household in this example is fictional.">
  <div class="learning-two-col"><section class="answer-panel"><p class="eyebrow">The question</p><h2>How do local retailer offers affect purchasing, and who can reach them?</h2><p>These are connected questions, but they measure different things. The example trials examine whether households buy a specified basket. The scenario examines whether a household can reach and afford that basket within its available time.</p><div class="answer-summary"><span class="result-kicker">Worked synthesis · purchasing</span><p>The fabricated trial data yield a pooled risk ratio of <strong>{overall.rr.toFixed(2)}</strong> for at least one basket purchase within four weeks. Removing the high-risk example trial reduces it to <strong>{foodSyntheses[1].rr.toFixed(2)}</strong>.</p><p class="small">This demonstrates an analysis, not an observed benefit. An effect above 1 here favours purchasing the specified basket; it says nothing directly about diet quality or health.</p></div><a class="button button-primary" href="/learning/food-access/effects/">Inspect the effect estimates <Icon/></a></section><aside class="question-facts"><p class="eyebrow">Scope of the worked comparison</p><dl><div><dt>Population</dt><dd>Fictional households individually assigned to offers at shared retailers</dd></div><div><dt>Intervention</dt><dd>Household-specific basket voucher and personalised product information</dd></div><div><dt>Comparison</dt><dd>Usual retailer offer</dd></div><div><dt>Outcome</dt><dd>At least one qualifying basket purchase in four weeks</dd></div><div><dt>Evidence</dt><dd>{overall.k} synthetic trials · {overall.totalParticipants.toLocaleString('en-GB')} households</dd></div></dl></aside></div>
  <section class="learning-section"><div class="section-heading"><div><p class="eyebrow">Inside the worked example</p><h2>Four ways to examine the question</h2></div></div><div class="learning-feature-grid"><a class="learning-feature" href="/learning/food-access/effects/"><span class="feature-number">01</span><h3>Compare the findings</h3><p>Inspect individual estimates, the defined pooled analysis and a prespecified sensitivity comparison.</p><span class="feature-link">Open the effects explorer <Icon/></span></a><a class="learning-feature" href="/learning/food-access/context/"><span class="feature-number">02</span><h3>Look at context and equity</h3><p>Read illustrative qualitative findings and identify information the quantitative studies do not provide.</p><span class="feature-link">Explore context <Icon/></span></a><a class="learning-feature" href="/learning/food-access/confidence/"><span class="feature-number">03</span><h3>Examine the assessment</h3><p>See why a numerical estimate is not a certainty judgement, and what a review team would need to assess.</p><span class="feature-link">Read the confidence profile <Icon/></span></a><a class="learning-feature" href="/learning/food-access/scenario/"><span class="feature-number">04</span><h3>Try a local scenario</h3><p>Change a fictional shop offer and opening hours. Compare access for households with different constraints.</p><span class="feature-link">Open the scenario lab <Icon/></span></a></div></section>
  <section class="learning-section provenance-section"><div><p class="eyebrow">From source to synthesis</p><h2>Keep the units of evidence clear.</h2><p>A document is not necessarily a separate study. This worked example links each trial to its outcome and the analyses that include it.</p></div><ol class="provenance-flow"><li><strong>Source sheet</strong><span>Invented methods and extracted counts</span></li><li><strong>Study</strong><span>One trial, one set of households</span></li><li><strong>Outcome</strong><span>Basket purchasing at four weeks</span></li><li><strong>Synthesis</strong><span>Explicit study membership and method</span></li></ol></section>
  <div class="learning-notice"><Icon name="info"/><div><strong>What this example does not establish</strong><p>There is no real review behind these six trials, no validated local forecast and no completed GRADE assessment. The scenario’s access calculations do not use the purchasing risk ratio as a behavioural parameter. Its inputs and simplifications are documented separately.</p></div></div>
  <div class="learning-endlinks"><a href="/data/food-analysis.json" download>Download the synthetic analysis <Icon name="download" size={17}/></a><a href="/evidence/">Return to the example library <Icon size={17}/></a></div>
</LearningLayout>
